- How would you extend this to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design a branchless bit-packing operation across 128 bits—a common low-level optimization used in compression algorithms, bitmap libraries, and SIMD data processing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit masks and shifts
- Branchless algorithms
- Parallel bit extraction and compression

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 compress128(uint128 x, uint128 mask) {
    // Stage 1: compact adjacent bits (shift 1)
    uint128 m = mask & ~(mask << 1);
    x = (x & m) | ((x >> 1) & ~m);
    mask &= ~(mask << 1);

    // Subsequent stages (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) follow the same pattern
    // with appropriate shift amounts...

    return x;
}
```

Why this is asked
- Tests ability to implement 128-bit rotation without branching, using a safe 128-bit intermediate. Covers edge cases n=0 and n=64 cleanly.

Key concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic, circular shift, branch-free implementation, handling wrap-around.

Code example
```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  __uint128_t full = ((__uint128_t)x.hi << 64) | x.lo;
  unsigned k = n & 127;
  __uint128_t r = (full << k) | (full >> (128 - k));
  uint128 out; out.hi = (uint64_t)(r >> 64); out.lo = (uint64_t)r; return out;
}
```

